Build Specification — App 10: Scan-Family Comparator
Completeness status: COMPLETE. All sections delivered in one pass. No truncation. Formatted for copy-paste into another conversation window. No tables, no boxes.

What It Is
A command-line tool that runs the same data under several scan families and reports calibration and power side by side. It runs W_adj, W_pair with and without a cap, and the endpoint-conditioned T_max, and reports the comparison. It takes in a pipeline specification, the data, and the families to compare, and puts out a side-by-side table of critical values, calibration, and power. It is for analysts choosing a scan family.

Core Requirements
Functional requirements:

Accept a pipeline specification produced by the Pipeline Specification Wizard (App 8).

Accept a dataset.

Accept a list of scan families to compare.

For each scan family, run the full-pipeline simulator (App 9) to compute the empirical null distribution.

For each scan family, compute the 95th-percentile critical value of the scan statistic.

For each scan family, compute the calibration at a pre-specified alpha (default 0.05) by simulating null datasets and checking the rejection rate.

For each scan family, compute the power against a specified alternative.

For each scan family, compute the rejection rates at the specified gap widths and depths.

Report the critical values, calibration, and power side by side.

Support the scan families W_adj, W_pair with and without a cap, and the endpoint-conditioned T_max.

Support the boundary conventions B1 and B2.

Support the empty-W conventions no_candidate and zero.

Support the tail definition, whether >= or >.

Support the multiplicity procedure.

Non-functional requirements:

Deterministic: same specification, data, seed produce same output.

Fast: run the comparison within a reasonable time (depends on the number of families, N, and the number of power simulations).

Versioned: the tool records the specification version and the comparator version.

Reproducible: the tool records the seed and the random number generator.

Input Specification
Input 1. Pipeline specification. Required. A JSON file conforming to the schema produced by the Pipeline Specification Wizard (App 8).

Input 2. Observed data. Required. A dataset, in CSV, JSON, or plain text.

Input 3. Scan families to compare. Required. A list of family identifiers, each with its parameters.

Input 4. Alternative specification. Required for power computation. A specification of the gap width and depth to test.

Input 5. Alpha. Optional. The nominal level for calibration. Default 0.05.

Input 6. Seed. Required. The random seed.

Input 7. Output directory. Required.

Input 8. Number of simulations. Optional. Overrides the N in the specification.

Input 9. Number of power simulations. Optional. The number of datasets per cell for the power computation. Default 1500.

Input 10. Output format. Optional. Values: text (default), json, both.

Output Specification
Output 1. Critical value table. Text or JSON. For each scan family, the 95th-percentile critical value of the scan statistic.

Output 2. Calibration table. Text or JSON. For each scan family, the rejection rate at nominal alpha over null datasets.

Output 3. Power table. Text or JSON. For each scan family, the rejection rates at the specified gap widths and depths.

Output 4. Side-by-side comparison. Text or JSON. The critical values, calibration, and power for all scan families in a single view.

Output 5. Summary report. Text. The recommended scan family based on the comparison, with the reasoning.

Output 6. Diagnostic plots. Optional. Power curves for each scan family.

Output 7. Reproducibility record. JSON. The specification version, comparator version, seed, RNG, number of simulations, and runtime.

Module-by-Module Specification
Module 1: Specification Loader
Reads the pipeline specification. Validates it against the schema. Rejects malformed specifications.

Module 2: Data Loader
Reads the observed data. Applies the transformation, jittering, and clustering specified. Assigns the support by the specified rule.

Module 3: Family Resolver
Reads the list of scan families to compare. Each family has an identifier and its parameters.

Supported families:

W_adj: the adjacent-order-statistic family. Parameters: g_min_value, g_min_units, boundary_convention, empty_w_convention.

W_pair: the general pair family. Parameters: g_min_value, g_min_units, p_max (optional), boundary_convention, empty_w_convention.

T_max: the endpoint-conditioned statistic of Section 6.1 of the Null-Model Protocol paper. Parameters: g_min_value, g_min_units, p_max (optional), boundary_convention, empty_w_convention.

W_pair with cap: W_pair with p_max set to a specific value.

W_pair without cap: W_pair with p_max set to null.

Module 4: Critical Value Calculator
For each family, runs the full-pipeline simulator (App 9) with the specification and the family parameters. Computes the empirical null distribution. Computes the 95th-percentile critical value.

Module 5: Calibration Calculator
For each family, simulates null datasets. For each dataset, computes the scan statistic. Checks whether the statistic exceeds the critical value at the pre-specified alpha. Computes the rejection rate.

Module 6: Power Calculator
For each family, simulates datasets with the specified alternative. For each dataset, computes the scan statistic. Checks whether the statistic exceeds the critical value at the pre-specified alpha. Computes the rejection rate.

The alternative specification includes the gap width, the gap depth, and the gap location.

Module 7: Comparison Table Builder
Assembles the critical value table, the calibration table, and the power table. Produces the side-by-side comparison.

Module 8: Recommendation Engine
Analyzes the comparison. Identifies the best scan family for the specified alternative. Produces a recommendation with the reasoning.

The recommendation may note that no scan family dominates: the choice depends on the alternative of interest and should be made in advance.

Module 9: Report Formatter
Produces the summary report and the diagnostic plots.

Module 10: Reproducibility Recorder
Records the specification version, comparator version, seed, RNG, number of simulations, and runtime.

Module 11: Command-Line Interface
Parses arguments. Invokes Modules 1 through 10. Writes the results to the output directory.

Full Computation Specification
6.1 Critical Value
For a scan family F and a scan statistic Lambda_max, the 95th-percentile critical value is the value c such that P(Lambda_max >= c) = 0.05 under the null. It is estimated by simulation: run N null datasets, compute Lambda_max for each, sort, and take the value at the 95th percentile.

For W_adj alone at n = 40, the critical value is approximately 14.0.

For W_pair uncapped at n = 40, the critical value is approximately 23.8.

For W_pair with the endpoint-corrected count at n = 40, the critical value is approximately 15.6.

For W_pair with p_max = 0.5 at n = 40, the critical value is approximately 14.7.

For W_pair with p_max = 0.5 and the endpoint correction at n = 40, the critical value is approximately 10.2.

For T_max with adjacent windows only, the critical value is approximately 6.67.

For T_max with all windows uncapped, the critical value is approximately 7.97.

For T_max with all windows and p_max = 0.5, the critical value is approximately 7.50.

6.2 Calibration
For each family, the calibration is the rejection rate at nominal alpha over null datasets. The simulator runs the full pipeline on each null dataset. The rejection rate is the fraction of datasets with Lambda_max >= critical value.

For the full pipeline, the rejection rate should be at or near the nominal alpha. For a mismatched pipeline (for example, ignoring the support rule), the rejection rate may be higher.

6.3 Power
For each family, the power is the rejection rate at nominal alpha over datasets with the specified alternative. The alternative is specified by:

The gap width w, as a fraction of the support or as a value in the transformed space.

The gap depth theta, as a fraction between 0 and 1.

The gap location, as a fraction of the support or as a value in the transformed space.

The rejection rate is the fraction of datasets with Lambda_max >= critical value.

The power computation uses the same N for each cell (default 1500). The Monte Carlo standard error is at most about 0.013 per cell.

